<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peninsulares</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peninsulares were Spaniards born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> who lived in the empire's possessions overseas, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a> or Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>. Higher offices in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> were held by peninsulares, who were distinguished from the criollos, Spaniards born in the New World, and from the insulares born in the Philippines. In some places and times, as during the wars of independence, they were called godos, '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>', in scorn, or in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> gachupines; godos is still used pejoratively for Spaniards in Chile and for peninsular Spaniards in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Hispanic America an upsurge of nationalism in the 1810s and 1820s, led by the criollos against the <b>peninsulares</b>, cost Spain nearly all its colonies.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Spaniards born in Spain, the <b>peninsulares</b>, were a small minority who held most of the highest offices.</blockquote><a href="/societies/spanish-america">Social Hierarchy of Spanish America</a></li></ul></section>
